# A type name is a keyword, and binding one is a compile error.
#
# `str`, `int`, `dict` and the rest name types, and `type(x)` answers with the
# very thing the name denotes — so `type(x) == dict` is Oro's one type test.
# That only holds if `dict` cannot also be a variable. It used to be able to:
# `dict = {}` was an ordinary assignment to an ordinary shadowable global, and
# after it `type(x) == dict` compared a value against an empty dict and read
# exactly like the test it was no longer performing. CPython has the same hole
# and lives with it; Oro is frozen, so it is closed here instead.
#
# CPython runs this file happily, which is the whole point: reviewed by hand,
# and the diagnostic is what wants reviewing if it changes. A compile error
# stops the program, so this file can hold nothing else. The other seven
# binding forms — `def`, `class`, a parameter, a `for` target, `import ... as`,
# `except ... as`, `global` — are refused the same way and are covered by
# `compiler::tests`.

print("this line never runs")

dict = {}
